@page "/serialized-scoped-dbcontext"
@rendermode @(new InteractiveServerRenderMode(prerender: false))

<PageTitle>Serialized shared DbContext</PageTitle>

<h1>Serialized shared DbContext</h1>

<p class="lead">
    This page keeps the same Blazor Server setup, the same scoped service idea, and the same injected scoped
    <code>AppDbContext</code>. The difference is that the service uses a <code>SemaphoreSlim</code> to ensure only one
    database operation touches the shared context at a time.
</p>

<div class="alert alert-info" role="alert">
    Expected result: all cards should succeed, and they should all show the <strong>same</strong> service ID and the
    <strong>same</strong> DbContext ID. The tradeoff is that the work is now queued instead of truly concurrent.
</div>

<div class="card border-info shadow-sm mb-4">
    <div class="card-body">
        <h2 class="h4">Why this is safe</h2>
        <p>
            The shared <code>DbContext</code> instance still exists, but the service no longer lets two operations use
            it at the same time. In other words, this pattern fixes the problem by <strong>serializing access</strong>,
            not by creating more contexts.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-0">
            This works only if <strong>every</strong> method that touches that shared context goes through the same
            gate. If one code path bypasses the gate, the concurrency issue can come back.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-2 row-cols-xl-3 g-4">
    <div class="col">
        <SerializedScopedUserLookupCard Title="Component A" UserId="1" />
